    '----------------------------- USAGE NOTES -----------------------------
    ' Instance Identifiers in Practice:
    '   - In Azure Digital Twins and NGSI-LD-based smart city deployments, the instance identifier ($dtId) is used as the primary label for entities and as the target in relationships.
    '   - $dtId is a globally unique string assigned to each digital twin instance; it is referenced directly in relationships and is the main node label in tools like ADT Explorer.
    '
    ' Denormalized String References:
    '   - NGSI-LD models often use denormalized string properties (e.g., areaServed) to reference related entities by name or identifier, in addition to formal graph relationships (e.g., Contains/Within from Saref4City or GeoSPARQL).
    '   - This pattern reflects a pragmatic approach: string references coexist with explicit graph topology, enabling flexible but potentially fragile cross-entity linking.
    '
    ' Ontology Usage Intent:
    '   - The ontology is designed to support both formal, navigable relationships (for graph traversal and reasoning) and denormalized references (for compatibility and ease of integration with existing systems and APIs).
    '   - These patterns are not mutually exclusive; both are present in real-world deployments and are supported by the ontology authors' design.
    '----------------------------------------------------------------------

    note as NoteDtId
    Each entity has a <b>$dtId</b>:
    * Used as the node label;
    * Reference in relationships.
    end note

    ' An administrative division, unit, entity, area or region,
    ' also referred to as a subnational entity, constituent unit, or country subdivision,
    ' is a portion of a country or other region delineated for the purpose of administration.
    ' (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Administrative_division)
    entity AdministrativeArea <<abstract>> extends Feature {
    }

    ' A district is a type of administrative division that, in some countries, is managed by local government.
    ' Across the world, areas known as 'districts' vary greatly in size, spanning regions
    ' or counties, several municipalities, subdivisions of municipalities, school district, or political district.
    ' (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/District)
    '
    ' <<bridge>> decision:
    ' - **`District`** — the Smart City side of the District ↔ SubGeographicalRegion semantic gap. A District is a
    '   civic/administrative zone defined by city governance, while SubGeographicalRegion is an electrical topology zone
    '   defined by grid infrastructure. Both partition the same physical city space but use entirely different boundaries
    '   and naming conventions. The agent must negotiate this mismatch — correlating a charging station's district
    '   membership with the SubGeographicalRegion (and its Substation) that provides its electrical supply.
    entity District <<bridge>> extends AdministrativeArea {
    }

    ' A neighbourhood (British English), or neighborhood (American English; see spelling differences),
    ' is a geographically localised community within a larger city, town, suburb or rural area.
    ' (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neighbourhood)
    '
    ' <<bridge>> decision:
    ' - **`Neighbourhood`** — finer-grained Smart City side of the same spatial gap as District. A Neighbourhood
    '   represents a sub-district community zone (e.g., a downtown block cluster), which may map more precisely to a
    '   single Substation service area than a full District does. When the agent resolves which transformer serves a
    '   charging station, Neighbourhood provides a more granular named anchor than District, reducing the spatial
    '   search to a smaller candidate set of UsagePoints and PowerTransformers.
    entity Neighbourhood <<bridge>> extends AdministrativeArea {
    }
}

package "Energy Grid Ontology" {
    ' | Stereotype | Meaning |
    ' |---|---|
    ' | `<<key>>` | Entities that are **directly read or compared** during the coordination decision — the agent must retrieve and reason about their properties to assign an EV to a charging slot |
    ' | `<<bridge>>` | The **cross-domain gap marker** — an entity that exists in one ontology but whose counterpart lives in the other; the semantic mapping the agent must negotiate |
    ' | `<<context>>` | Entities that provide **structural topology** — necessary to traverse the graph and locate the key entities, but not themselves decision variables |
    ' | `<<abstract>>` | **Base classes** from the CIM hierarchy — present only to satisfy inheritance chains; carry no scenario-specific semantics |
    ' | `<<open-reference>>` | An entity **referenced by this ontology but defined in another** — an incomplete cross-domain slot the agent must resolve through reasoning rather than graph traversal |

